# Arch packages do not enable services for you, and a user service could not be
# enabled from here anyway — there is no user at pacman time. So this says what
# to run, once, and then gets out of the way.

post_install() {
  cat <<'EOF'

PC GamePak is installed. One command to start watching for cartridges:

    systemctl --user enable --now pc-gamepak-watcher.service

That runs a small process (about 2 MB) that wakes when a drive is mounted,
checks it for a cartridge.conf, and opens the launcher on it. No root, no udev
rule.

A cartridge nothing mounted is a cartridge the watcher never hears about, and
not every desktop automounts one: GNOME does, KDE asks first unless told
otherwise, and a bare window manager does nothing at all. So the watcher pulls
in pc-gamepak-automount.service, which runs udiskie against the same udisks2
your desktop uses. Install udiskie and it just works:

    pacman -S --asdeps udiskie

If your desktop already automounts, skip it — the Want fails quietly and
nothing else changes.

Write a cartridge with the wizard, from your menu or:

    pc-gamepak --create

Check one against its manifest:

    pc-gamepak-verify /run/media/you/CARTRIDGE

If you would rather have nothing resident, the udev route is in
/usr/share/doc/pc-gamepak/system-install/ — it needs root and bakes your
username into a systemd unit, which is why it is not what this package installs.

EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
  cat <<'EOF'

If the watcher is enabled, restart it to pick up the new binary:

    systemctl --user restart pc-gamepak-watcher.service

EOF
}

pre_remove() {
  # Best effort: the service belongs to a user, and pacman runs as root, so
  # this cannot reach into every session. Told, rather than done silently.
  cat <<'EOF'

Remember to stop the watcher in each user session that enabled it:

    systemctl --user disable --now pc-gamepak-watcher.service

EOF
}
